  - Same for a missing [38;5;153m[data-error-for][39m container when the default renderer is in use — otherwise errors are computed
    and then displayed nowhere. Supplying [38;5;153moptions.renderErrors[39m opts out of that check.
  - If [38;5;153monValid[39m throws or rejects, [38;5;153mbindForm[39m catches it and renders the message as the form-level error. That's what gives
    a failed login somewhere to surface instead of the console.

  [1mTesting.[0m [38;5;153mrules.js[39m and [38;5;153mvalidate.js[39m are pure functions with no imports — they test in plain Node with zero setup, which
  is the payoff for the split in Section 1. I'd use [1m[38;5;153mnode:test[0m, the built-in runner: [38;5;153mnpm test[39m → [38;5;153mnode --test[39m, no
  dependencies added, works with ESM natively.

  Coverage: each rule's pass and fail cases, the empty-value convention, [38;5;153mmatches[39m against another field, first-error-wins
  ordering, and a valid/invalid schema end to end.

  [38;5;153mbind-form.js[39m is the one piece needing a DOM. Rather than pull in jsdom for this alone, I'd verify it manually in the
  browser (submit empty, submit partial, fix a field and watch the error clear, successful submit) and keep the
  automated tests on the logic. If you'd rather have it covered automatically, that's one devDependency — say so and
  I'll include it.

